Files here: the ten parts' STEP models (N.step) and G-code programs (N.ngc), unchanged, as
downloaded 2026-11-24 (each checked against the record's MD5; SHA-256 in SHA256SUMS). The
record's rendered images of the machined parts are not copied.

From the record's description (not ours): all parts are made from a raw block of 80 x 80 x 50 mm
with one end mill of at most 6 mm diameter, single setup, on a three-axis machine; the programs
were made with Autodesk CAM (their header: "HERSTELLER AUTODESK", "MODEL GENERIC 3-AXIS").

What we read off the programs (verify/forge-cam-quality, --ngc, --part): G54 is the corner of
the CAM stock, the part's top is at Z -1 (every program faces 1 mm off at Z -1; ZMIN in each tool
comment is the part's deepest floor below Z -1), and the part sits inside the stock by 0.5 mm per
side for parts 1-4 (stock 81 x 81: the arc centres of part 3's holes are at 15.5 and 65.5, the
model's at -25 and 25) and by 1 mm for parts 5-6 (82 x 82: centres 23.5/58.5 and 13.5/68.5).
Checked: with these frames each program's end state over the part's footprint is the part, to
within the cells straddling its walls (forge-cam-quality README, "like for like"). Parts 7-10:
taken as 81 x 81, not checked. These are our readings, not the authors' statements: their
description gives the raw block (80 x 80 x 50), not the CAM stock or where G54 is.

Used here as an outside reference: the same parts, another CAM system's toolpaths, measured with
the same model as forge-cam's own (verify/forge-cam-quality/README.md, "Foreign programs").
